Neural networks started its journey with a fundamental theorem which guarantees that it works. So let us start there as well.


Universal Approximation Theorem
The Universal Approximation Theorem states that a feed forward neural network with a single hidden layer and a non‑polynomial activation function (such as sigmoid or ReLU) can approximate any continuous function on a compact domain to arbitrary accuracy, given sufficient width.
simply, given that we have enough number of neurons in the hidden layer, the neural network can approximate any continuous function on a closed neighbourhood.
Now, here are the questions that came to my mind when I read the above statement.

Why does the Universal Approximation Theorem require a non-polynomial (typically nonlinear) activation function?
How does increasing the number of neurons in the hidden layer improve a neural network’s ability to approximate a function?



1. Why non-linear activation functions?
This is fairly the simplest question among the 3 above. For this, lets take a reference neural network with just 2 neurons in its hidden layer but no activation functions at all.


