        node_labels=[['x'], ['h₁', 'h₂'], ['ŷ']],
        edge_labels=weights,
        biases=biases, legend=False)









As there is no activation function, then,

h_{1} = w_{11}x + b_{1} \\
h_{2} = w_{12}x + b_{2}

each of the hidden nodes is actually a straight line (as x is one dimensional here). So this makes,

\hat{y} = w_{21}h_{1} + w_{22}h_{2} + b_{3}

Substituting h_{1} and h_{2},

\hat{y} = w_{21}(w_{11}x + b_{1}) + w_{22}(w_{12}x + b_{2}) + b_{3}

Expanding,

\hat{y} = w_{21}w_{11}x + w_{21}b_{1} + w_{22}w_{12}x + w_{22}b_{2} + b_{3}

Collecting terms in x,

\hat{y} = \underbrace{(w_{21}w_{11} + w_{22}w_{12})}_{W_{\text{eff}}}\,x
        + \underbrace{(w_{21}b_{1} + w_{22}b_{2} + b_{3})}_{b_{\text{eff}}}

which is just

\hat{y} = W_{\text{eff}}\,x + b_{\text{eff}}

— another straight line. It doesn’t matter how many nodes you add to the hidden layer, or how many hidden layers you stack: without a non-linear activation, the whole network collapses to a single affine map (straight line for one dimension, hyperplane for higher dimensions). And as you have probably guessed already, one straight line can’t approximate any arbitrary continuous function to any accuracy we like.


2. Why is a wider neural network better?
As we have understood why non-linear or non-polynomial functions in the nodes are needed, the next question why and how adding a node with non-linear activation function makes the network a better approximator.
To understand this, we shall do a case study, in which we shall take an example of a simple enough function and will actually try to approximate it by training a shallow neural network.
Lets take the function as - 
