Okay, what just happended? We have 8 times more neurons now, as you can see as well above, we have far more kinks (20 to be exact for 20 relu nodes), but still it does not look like the fit is getting any better. So, is universal approximation theorem wrong or we are missing something?
See, for any continuous function, UAT confirms that there always exists a neural network which can fit that function to a given level of accuracy. But UAT does not guarantee that we shall always find it. Finding the best fit parameters depends on multiple things, for example which optimization algorithm we are using to minimize the loss , or from which point we are starting our loss minimization journey.
Pytorch by default uses a kaiming-uniform style parameter initialization, which makes the weights and biases to be initialized randomly from the uniform distribution U\left(-\frac{1}{\sqrt{n_{\mathrm{in}}}},\frac{1}{\sqrt{n_{\mathrm{in}}}}\right) . For our case the input is 1-dimensional, so n_{\mathrm{in}}=1, hence,

w \sim U(-1,1), \qquad b \sim U(-1,1)

