As you can see from the distribution, the 90% of the times the parameters are initialized in such a way so that the hinges lie in between [-5,5]. so essentially, outside this boundary, the function is affine and has no way to bend and learn the non-linearity.
Quick fix to this? we can initialize the parameters so that the hinges get unformly distributed.


Show code
import copy

def uniform_knots(model, lo=XLO, hi=XHI):
    """Return a copy of `model` with every hinge on a uniform grid over [lo, hi].

    We want -b_i / w_i = k_i, so we keep the weights (and hence the slopes)
    exactly as Kaiming gave them, and just solve for the bias: b_i = -w_i k_i."""
    model_ = copy.deepcopy(model)
    with torch.no_grad():
        L = model_.layers[0]
        k = lo + (hi - lo) * (torch.arange(L.out_features) + 0.5) / L.out_features
        L.bias.copy_(-L.weight[:, 0] * k)
    return model_


model = FFN(hidden_dims=[20])
model_reinitiated = uniform_knots(model)


fig, model = run((x_train, y_train), (x_val, y_val), model=model_reinitiated, hidden_dims=[20], epochs=3500)
fig.show()


